discussion_comments.comment_body:
**[Critical]** Neither skip path ever calls `TodoLists.done()`, so skipped todos stay `pending` on GitLab forever and are re-fetched on every single poll.

Only the success path marks a todo done (line 178). Both this branch (unsupported `target_type`) and the `!template` branch below advance `lastProcessedAt` and `continue` without draining the todo. That covers every `assigned`, `review_requested`, `build_failed`, `approval_required`, `marked` and Epic/Design todo — plus (per the `directly_addressed` comment above) the most common mention shape.

Reproduced: an `assigned` todo and an `Epic` todo both produced `done calls: []`.

Combined with the unbounded pagination in `TodoLists.all` (see the comment at line 139), the per-poll cost grows monotonically for the life of the channel and never drains. There is also no allowlist gate in front of it — `SenderGate` only runs inside `handleInbound`, long after the fetch — so anyone who can assign the bot or trigger an untemplated action inflates the pending list. The bot account's GitLab To-Do page also becomes permanently unusable for its human owner.

The GitHub sibling drains unconditionally via `markNotificationsAsRead`.

```js
try {
  await this.api.TodoLists.done({ todoId: todo.id });
} catch (err) {
  process.stderr.write(
    `[Channel:${this.name}] failed to clear skipped todo ${todo.id}: ${err}\n`,
  );
}
this.cursor.lastProcessedAt = todo.updated_at;
```

