discussion_comments.comment_body:
**[Critical]** None of the three `.all()` calls bound pagination, so each poll walks the *entire* pending-todo list and each todo re-downloads the *entire* note history of its issue/MR.

Confirmed in the vendored client (`@gitbeaker/core@42.5.0`, `dist/index.mjs:64-83`): `getManyMore` recursively follows the `Link: next` header until exhaustion, and the only escape hatches are `page` (single page) or `maxPages` **and** `perPage` together —

```js
const withinBounds = maxPages && perPage ? newAcc.length / +perPage < maxPages : true;
```

Neither is passed here or at lines 204/208, so at GitLab's default `per_page=20`: a 600-comment MR costs ~30 sequential GETs *per todo, per poll*, and the pending list costs `ceil(P/20)` GETs where `P` only ever grows (see the comment at line 152). `sort: 'asc'` makes the notes case strictly worse — the wanted notes are the newest, so full pagination is mandatory to reach them — and ~98% of what is transferred is discarded client-side at line 144 / 213-219.

The GitHub sibling bounds both server-side: `listNotifications({ since, per_page: 100 })` and `listComments({ since: windowSince, per_page: 100 })`.

GitLab genuinely has no `created_after` on these endpoints, but the fix is bounded pagination, not unbounded:
```js
// todos
await this.api.TodoLists.all({ state: 'pending', perPage: 100, maxPages: 5 });
// notes — newest-first, stop at the window bound
await this.api.IssueNotes.all(chatId, todo.target.iid, {
  sort: 'desc', orderBy: 'created_at', perPage: 100, maxPages: 2,
});
```

